.TH GIT-GROVE 1 "2026-08-21" "git-grove 0.5.0" "Git Extensions"
.SH NAME
git-grove \- manage repositories as a bare clone surrounded by Git worktrees
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git grove
.RB [ --ignore-unsupported ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.PP
.B git-grove
.RB [ --ignore-unsupported ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A grove is a directory holding a bare repository in
.BR .bare ,
a
.B .git
pointer file, format metadata in
.B grove.*
keys in
.BR .bare/config ,
an
.B AGENTS.md
guide with
.B CLAUDE.md
beside it, and one directory per Git worktree.
.PP
Inside a grove, invocation without a command runs
.BR list .
A repository locator in command position selects
.BR clone .
The command aliases are
.BR plant = clone ,
.BR seed = init ,
.BR transplant = adopt " (hidden),"
.BR sprout = add ,
.BR survey = list ,
.BR tend = sync ,
and
.BR propagate = publish .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B clone \fR[\fB-b\fR|\fB--branch \fIbranch\fR] \fIurl\fR [\fIdir\fR] [\fB--\fR \fIgit-options\fR...]
Clone a repository as
.I dir/.bare
and create its first worktree.  The default directory is derived from the
repository locator.  The selected branch also names the first worktree.
Options following
.B --
are forwarded only when they preserve the grove layout and repository
identity.  Options which choose another Git directory, worktree, object
directory, configuration overlay, mirror layout, separate Git directory, or
unsafe reference relationship are refused.  A reference repository requires
.BR --dissociate .
.TP
.B init \fR[\fB-b\fR|\fB--branch \fIbranch\fR] [\fIdir\fR]
Create an empty repository directly as a grove.  The destination must be
missing or an existing empty directory.  If a branch is supplied it becomes
the unborn initial branch and the first worktree name; otherwise Git's initial
branch setting is used.
.TP
.B adopt \fR[\fB--remote \fIname\fR] [\fB--default-branch \fIbranch\fR] [\fIpath\fR]
Convert an ordinary repository in place into a grove without fetching or
checking out over its current files.  The repository must have a real
.B .git
directory, exactly one worktree, and quiescent state: no operation marker,
lock, conflict stage, sparse checkout, initialized submodule, hard-linked
payload file, nested mount boundary, or ambiguous remote/default choice.
Raw path bytes, index and staged state, ignored and untracked files, private
Git state, references, reflogs, and configuration are preserved.
.RS
.PP
.B --remote
selects the configured remote recorded in grove metadata.
.B --default-branch
selects the default worktree when it cannot be derived unambiguously.
The current checkout becomes the payload worktree; a distinct default branch
is materialized as a second worktree.
.PP
Before layout mutation, adopt durably creates exactly one
.B .grove-adopt-<nonce>/
transaction.  An interrupted run prints the exact recovery commands:
.PP
.B git grove adopt --continue \fIpath
.br
.B git grove adopt --abort \fIpath
.PP
Recovery validates the journal, root identity, and exact before/after evidence.
It refuses corrupt, foreign, or ambiguous evidence and never overwrites manual
edits.  The hidden compatibility alias is
.BR transplant .
The test-only
.B GIT_GROVE_FAILPOINT
environment variable is inert in release builds.
.RE
.TP
.B add \fIbranch\fR [\fIdir\fR]
Add a worktree for an existing local branch or a branch found through exactly
one remote fetch refspec.  If
.I dir
is omitted, the branch name is used as the worktree path.
.TP
.B add \fR[\fB--start \fIrevision\fR] \fIbranch\fR [\fIdir\fR]
Create a branch from
.I revision
when it exists neither locally nor on a remote.  In an unborn repository, an
absent
.B --start
creates an orphan branch.  The option is refused if the branch already exists.
.TP
.B add \fB--detach \fIrevision\fR [\fIdir\fR]
Add a detached worktree.  The default directory is
.BR detached- <short-object-name>.
.TP
.B list \fR[\fB--porcelain\fR]
Show each non-bare worktree, branch, upstream, ahead/behind state, dirtiness,
and lock or in-progress state.  A pending adoption transaction blocks the
command entirely, before any row is produced, and exits with status
.BR 2 .
Human output escapes non-printing bytes reversibly.  Missing, invalid, or
prunable worktrees are reported and cause exit status
.BR 2 .
.TP
.B sync
Fetch every remote a registered branch worktree's upstream requires, one
atomic fetch per remote, never
.BR "fetch --all" .
If any required fetch fails, no worktree is updated and the command exits
with status
.BR 1 .
After all required fetches succeed, fast-forward only a worktree that is
clean and behind: no local commits ahead of its upstream, no uncommitted
changes, and no lock or in-progress operation.  Worktrees are updated one at
a time, in a stable order sorted by worktree path.  The only update command
run is
.BR "git merge --ff-only --no-edit --no-autostash --no-overwrite-ignore <upstream-oid>" ,
targeting the exact upstream commit re-inspected immediately beforehand, not
the symbolic
.B @{upstream}
revision, which Git would instead resolve from live branch configuration at
merge time.
.B sync
never rebases, pushes, invokes
.BR git-town ,
or resolves a conflict; a refused or blocked merge is reported and later
worktrees are still processed.  Any worktree left behind, blocked, or
otherwise unresolved causes exit status
.BR 2 .
The alias is
.BR tend .
.TP
.B publish \fR[\fB--remote \fIname\fR] [\fB--all-branches\fR] \fIurl\fR
.TQ
.B publish --create \fIowner\fB/\fIname\fR --host \fR{\fBgithub\fR|\fBgitlab\fR} [\fB--public\fR] [\fB--remote \fIname\fR] [\fB--all-branches\fR]
Give an unpublished grove a remote and push it, as a transaction with four
states \(em
.BR unpublished ,
.BR creating ,
.BR publishing ,
and
.BR published \(em
recorded in
.BR grove.publishState .
Its receipt,
.B grove.publishRemote
and
.BR grove.publishUrl ,
is written before the first step that mutates the remote or the local remote
configuration, and is never rolled back.  The read-only inspection of the
target therefore runs first, so a grove refused while the target is being
inspected is left untouched and can simply be republished to a corrected URL.
.RS
.PP
.B --remote
names the remote to create and defaults to
.BR origin .
.B --all-branches
publishes every local branch rather than the default branch alone.
.PP
.B --create
first creates the hosting-side repository through
.B "gh repo create"
or
.BR "glab repo create" ,
never a provider API, then publishes to it exactly as a bare
.B "publish \fIurl\fR"
would once the URL exists.
.I owner\fB/\fIname
must parse as exactly two non-empty, slash-separated components, checked
before anything is read from disk or the network.
.B --host
selects the provider and is required with
.BR --create ;
.B github
always targets
.BR github.com ", " gitlab " always targets " gitlab.com \(em
self-hosted and enterprise instances of either are out of scope, and neither
is inferred from a locally configured host.  The created repository is
private unless
.B --public
is given, which requires
.BR --create .
.PP
Before creating: the installed
.B gh
or
.B glab
version is checked against a declared minimum, refused at exit status
.B 64
if older; the same local checks a bare
.B publish
performs (a commit to publish, an existing default branch, a valid remote
name); and authentication, checked read-only with
.B "gh auth status"
or
.BR "glab auth status" ,
refused at exit status
.B 2
as "could not confirm authentication" rather than "not authenticated," since
both commands validate a stored token over the network.  While the hosting
side is being created, the grove records the requested provider, owner, name,
and remote in
.BR grove.publishProvider ,
.BR grove.publishOwner ,
.BR grove.publishName ,
and
.B grove.publishRemote
under the transient
.B creating
state; a rerun of
.B "publish --create"
against a grove already in
.B creating
resumes the same request without creating anything twice, and a bare
.B "publish \fIurl\fR"
against such a grove refuses at exit status
.BR 2 ,
naming the recorded owner/name, rather than silently reinterpreting it.  If
the hosting side already has a same-named, empty repository matching what
this grove recorded creating \(em typically an earlier attempt that created
it but did not finish publishing \(em publication proceeds using it, rather
than failing with "already exists."  A repository found under that name but
not matching what this grove recorded is refused at exit status
.B 2
as an unrelated existing repository.
.B --create
never asks a provider to delete, rename, or transfer a repository, including
one it created itself.
.PP
The target is inspected with
.B "git ls-remote --symref"
before anything is configured, and a branch already present there is compared
through a probe ref this transaction owns under
.BR refs/grove/publish-probe/ ,
deleted after the decision.
.B publish
never force-pushes, never rewrites history, never merges a host-created
commit, and never deletes a remote reference; only
.B --create
ever calls a hosting provider, and never to delete, rename, or transfer
anything.  A target whose default branch has diverged from this grove's, or whose history
is unrelated to it, and a target whose
.B HEAD
names another branch, are each refused with exit status
.BR 2 .
.PP
The push is a single
.B "git push --atomic"
invocation, carrying one refspec per published branch.  If the receiving end
does not advertise atomic push, Git refuses before sending any reference
update; the command reports that and exits with status
.BR 2 ,
and nothing is published.
.B --all-branches
is one atomic push precisely so a rejected branch cannot leave the others
half-published.  Upstream tracking is written explicitly through
.B "git config"
and verified, never through
.BR "push --set-upstream" .
.PP
Success is reported only after re-asking the hosting side whether its
.B HEAD
resolves to this grove's default branch.  If it does not, the branch is pushed
but the grove stays in the publishing state and the command exits with status
.BR 2 ,
asking for the hosting side's default branch to be set by hand; a rerun then
completes the transaction.  A rerun naming a different URL or remote name than
the receipt records is refused with exit status
.BR 2 ,
naming both values.  Comparison is exact and byte for byte.
.PP
There is no
.BR "publish --abort" .
A grove stuck in the
.B publishing
state, whose target can never accept an atomic push, is recovered by
unsetting
.BR grove.publishState ,
.BR grove.publishRemote ,
and
.B grove.publishUrl
by hand; see
.UR https://github.com/e-kulikov/git-grove/wiki/Getting-Out-Of-The-Publishing-State
.UE
for the walkthrough.
.B publish
does not rewrite
.BR AGENTS.md .
.RE
.PP
The alias is
.BR propagate .
.TP
.B completion \fR{\fBbash\fR|\fBzsh\fR|\fBfish\fR}
Write completion source for the selected shell to standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --ignore-unsupported
Report unsafe Git environment findings and consent to continuing with those
variables removed from child Git processes.  On a terminal, continuation also
requires the exact response
.B y
or
.BR yes ;
with nonterminal input, the flag itself records consent.  It does not bypass
the platform check, minimum Git version, or refused clone options.
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Show command help.
.TP
.BR -V , " --version"
Show the program version.
.SH GROVE LAYOUT
.TP
.B .bare/
The bare repository and shared Git administration directory.
.TP
.B .git
A regular pointer file whose content is exactly
.BR "gitdir: ./.bare" .
.TP
.B .bare/config
The real Git configuration also stores metadata under
.BR grove.version ,
.BR grove.defaultBranch ,
.BR grove.remote ,
.BR grove.publishState ,
.BR grove.publishRemote ,
.BR grove.publishUrl ,
.BR grove.publishProvider ,
.BR grove.publishOwner ,
and
.BR grove.publishName .
The last three are present only for a grove published through
.BR "publish --create" ,
and are never cleared once written, even after publication completes.
Version 0.4 refuses a newer
.B grove.version
before writing;
.B "publish --create"
adds new configuration keys, not a new layout revision.
.TP
.BR AGENTS.md " and " CLAUDE.md
Generated repository facts and command guidance.  The latter is a relative
symbolic link to the former.
.TP
.I worktree directories
Each managed worktree is a strict descendant of the grove root.  Final path
components must be vacant regular path names, not symbolic links.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Lifecycle commands require 64-bit Linux and Git 2.47 or newer.
.PP
Variables which redirect the repository, index, worktree, object database,
namespace, or configuration are refused before mutation.  These include
.BR GIT_DIR ,
.BR GIT_COMMON_DIR ,
.BR GIT_WORK_TREE ,
.BR GIT_INDEX_FILE ,
.BR GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY ,
.BR GIT_ALTERNATE_OBJECT_DIRECTORIES ,
.BR GIT_NAMESPACE ,
.BR GIT_CONFIG ,
.BR GIT_CONFIG_COUNT ,
.BR GIT_CONFIG_PARAMETERS ,
and numbered
.B GIT_CONFIG_KEY_*
and
.B GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_*
overlays.  Regardless of an override, all of them are removed from every Git
process started by git-grove.  Transport variables such as
.B GIT_SSH_COMMAND
are preserved.  Any Git child whose stderr git-grove parses to decide what to
do next is additionally spawned with
.B LC_ALL=C
and an empty
.BR LANGUAGE ,
so that stderr is untranslated regardless of the caller's own locale; other
Git children keep the caller's locale.
.PP
.B "publish --create"
pins
.B GH_HOST
or
.B GITLAB_HOST
to the selected provider's fixed host on the
.B gh
or
.B glab
child's own environment only, regardless of any value already set for either
variable, and explicitly clears
.B GH_REPO
on that child.  Credentials such as
.B GH_TOKEN
and
.B GITLAB_TOKEN
are left untouched and inherited normally.
.SH PORCELAIN OUTPUT
.B list --porcelain
starts with the NUL-terminated identifier
.BR git-grove-list-v1 .
Each worktree is a sequence of NUL-terminated key/value pairs followed by an
empty key.  The keys, in order, are
.BR worktree ,
.BR status ,
.BR branch ,
.BR upstream ,
.BR ahead ,
.BR behind ,
.BR dirty ,
and
.BR locked .
Paths and Git names are emitted as their original bytes.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Unexpected Git, filesystem, standard-output, or other I/O failure.
.TP
.B 2
Repository state needs a human decision; for example, ambiguous remote
branches, an occupied destination, or an invalid worktree record.
.TP
.B 64
Usage error, refused option or environment, unsupported context without an
override, or no grove where one is required.
.PP
During adoption, handled SIGINT, SIGTERM, and SIGHUP are forwarded to the
active Git child and exit as 128 plus the signal number after durable
reconciliation (130, 143, and 129 respectively).  SIGKILL leaves recovery to
an explicit
.B --continue
or
.BR --abort .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I git-grove_0.5.0_linux_x86_64.tar.gz
Static Linux x86_64 release archive containing the executable, license,
README, this manual, and generated Bash, Zsh, and Fish completions.
.TP
.I SHA256SUMS
SHA-256 checksum file for the release archive.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR git-clone (1),
.BR git-worktree (1)
